edges:
- all the same height :: len(set(h for row in grid for h in row)) == 1 :: max_increase_keeping_skyline([[2, 2], [2, 2]])
- a 2 × 2 grid :: len(grid) == 2 :: max_increase_keeping_skyline([[1, 2], [3, 4]])
- blocks with height 0 :: any(h == 0 for row in grid for h in row) :: max_increase_keeping_skyline([[0, 5], [5, 0]])
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(n²) | space=O(n)
complexity_note: n is the number of rows (and of columns).
why_time: One pass over the n × n cells finds the tallest in each row and column, and a second pass works out each building's rise in O(1): O(n²). Scanning the row and column again for each building would be O(n³).
why_space: Two lists of n heights: the tallest in each row and in each column.
hint: A building can't go above the tallest in its row, or the view from the side changes, and it can't go above the tallest in its column, or the view from the front changes.
hint: So it can rise to `min(row_top[r], col_top[c])`. Work out the lists `row_top` and `col_top` in one pass over the grid, before anything else.
hint: Then, in a second pass, add `min(row_top[r], col_top[c]) - grid[r][c]` for every cell.
right: Every building's limit depends on its row's tallest and its column's tallest, which are the same for all the buildings in that row or column. Working them out once, in one pass, makes each building's rise two list reads: O(n²) instead of O(n³).
