hint: If c points are at the same distance from i, any of them can be j and any of the other c − 1 can be k: add `c * (c - 1)` for every count.
right: Taking each point in turn as i turns "every group of three" into "count the others by distance", one pass per point. c points at the same distance give c × (c − 1) ordered pairs, so the total is O(n²) instead of O(n³), and squared distances keep every comparison exact.
